# gap[066]. A file-scope global that a FUNCTION WRITES.
#
# Two defects met here, one per backend, and fixing the loud one exposed the
# quiet one.
#
# `--backend=c` could not build this program at all: the DECLARATION emitter
# spelled the storage `duo_g_g` (it guarded on an empty module prefix) and the
# initializer-ASSIGNMENT emitter spelled it `duo_g__g` (it did not), so clang
# reported "use of undeclared identifier 'duo_g__g'". Two mangling rules for
# one symbol. There is now ONE mangler, `global_c_symbol` in `src/codegen.zig`,
# and every emitter routes through it.
#
# `--backend=direct` compiled it and answered WRONG. The native-scalar profile
# gives a file-scope binding no storage — each function body treats the name as
# its own register-resident local — so the write landed nowhere the next read
# could see, and the read folded to the initializer:
#
#     for i = 1, 3 / g = g + i    c printed 6    direct printed 3
#     for i = 1, 3 / g = g + 1    c printed 3    direct printed 0
#
# Exit 0 both times, `ok compile` both times, no diagnostic. The gap file
# recorded `direct 3` as "runs correctly", which it is not — 1+2+3 is 6. That
# misreading survived because nothing compared the VALUE against the oracle.
# A `while` loop with the identical body did it too, so it was never a `for`
# lowering defect.
#
# The direct backend now refuses with DNB001 (`mod-global-written:total`),
# which is why this program lives in `unsupported/`: an honest capability gap,
# not a miscompilation. A READ-ONLY file-scope binding still lowers natively —
# folding its initializer is correct when nothing can change it — so only the
# written case is refused, and the native census is unchanged.
#
# Promote this file into the corpus proper the day the native profile gives a
# written file-scope binding real storage. The C column is already correct and
# prints `6 5`.

total: i64 = 0
count: i64 = 5

bump(): i64
    for i = 1, 3
        total += i
    0

main(): i64
    bump()
    print(total)
    print(count)
    total
